---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How Much Blown-In Insulation Do I Need for My Attic? | CalcForDIY"
  description="Calculate how many bags of blown fiberglass or cellulose insulation you need to hit R-38, R-49, or R-60 attic depth per energy codes, including machine rental hours and soffit vent baffles."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "How Much Blown-In Insulation Do I Need for My Attic?",
    "description": "Comprehensive guide on calculating blown-in attic insulation bags, depth in inches, climate zone R-values, machine rental time, and soffit baffles.",
    "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "CalcForDIY Engineering" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "CalcForDIY", "url": "https://calcfordiy.com" }
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How many bags of blown-in insulation do I need for a 1,000 sq ft attic?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "For a 1,000 sq ft attic upgrading to R-49 depth (~16.5 inches), you need 25 to 28 bags of AttiCat blown fiberglass (or ~32 bags of Greenfiber cellulose) if starting from an uninsulated floor."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What R-value does my attic need by climate zone?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Under US Department of Energy standards, Climate Zones 1 to 3 (Southern US) require R-38 to R-49. Climate Zones 4 to 8 (Northern US and Canada) require R-49 to R-60 (approximately 16.5 to 20 inches of blown insulation)."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I blow new insulation over existing attic insulation?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes! You can blow loose-fill fiberglass or cellulose directly over existing fiberglass batts or old cellulose, provided the old insulation is dry, mold-free, and not compressed. Do not use faced batts on top of existing insulation."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4 print:hidden">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <span>Attic Blown-In Insulation Sizing</span>
  </div>

  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">How Much Blown-In Insulation Do I Need for My Attic?</h1>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5">
      <div class="p-5 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-blue-950 mt-0 mb-2">⚡ Quick Answer Summary:</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-900 text-base leading-relaxed mb-0">
          For a standard <strong>1,000 sq ft attic</strong> upgrading to <strong>R-49 code depth</strong> (approx. 16.5" settled depth):
          <br />• <strong>AttiCat Blown Fiberglass:</strong> ~27 bags (Yields ~37 sq ft / bag @ R-49)
          <br />• <strong>Greenfiber Loose-Fill Cellulose:</strong> ~34 bags (Yields ~29 sq ft / bag @ R-49)
          <br /><em>Blowing 27–34 bags takes approximately <strong>2 to 3 hours</strong> using a free 24-hour rental blowing machine from Home Depot or Lowe's.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Upgrading your attic insulation is one of the highest-ROI home improvements you can make, capable of cutting heating and cooling bills by up to 20%. However, calculating exact bag counts requires understanding climate zone R-values, settled depth in inches, and manufacturer coverage charts.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-950 mb-2 mt-0">Calculate Your Exact Attic Insulation Takeoff</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-900 mb-4">Enter your attic square footage, current R-value depth, and target climate zone to calculate bag counts, machine rental hours, and soffit baffles instantly.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/attic-insulation" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-6 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Go to the Blown Attic Insulation Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">1. Climate Zone R-Value Requirements</h2>
      <p>
        The US Department of Energy divides the country into 8 climate zones. The higher the R-value, the greater the resistance to heat flow:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse border border-gray-200 text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-100 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-900">
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Climate Zone &amp; Region</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Recommended R-Value</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Blown Fiberglass Depth</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Blown Cellulose Depth</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Zone 1 &ndash; 2 (Miami, Houston, Phoenix)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">R-38</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">13.0 inches</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">10.5 inches</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-blue-50">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Zone 3 &ndash; 4 (Atlanta, Dallas, Seattle, DC)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">R-49</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">16.5 inches</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">13.5 inches</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Zone 5 &ndash; 8 (Chicago, Denver, Boston, Minneapolis)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">R-60</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">20.0 inches</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">16.5 inches</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">2. Blown-In Bag Coverage Table (1,000 Sq Ft Attic)</h2>
      <p>
        Below is the exact bag count required to insulate a 1,000 sq ft attic space from R-0 (bare attic floor) vs. topping off an existing R-19 batt layer:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse border border-gray-200 text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-100 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-900">
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Target R-Value</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">AttiCat Fiberglass (From R-0)</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">AttiCat (Top-Off from R-19)</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Cellulose (From R-0)</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Blower Rental Time</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">R-30 (10" Depth)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">18 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">7 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">21 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-xs text-gray-600">1.5 Hours</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-blue-50">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">R-49 (16.5" Depth - Standard)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">27 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">16 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">34 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-xs text-blue-700 font-semibold">2 to 3 Hours</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">R-60 (20" Depth - Cold Zone)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">34 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">23 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">42 bags</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-xs text-gray-600">3 to 4 Hours</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">3. Step-by-Step Calculation Formula</h2>
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 space-y-3 font-mono text-sm">
        <p><strong>Step 1: Determine Net Attic Area</strong> = Attic Length &times; Width (e.g., 40' &times; 25' = 1,000 sq ft)</p>
        <p><strong>Step 2: Determine R-Value Deficit</strong> = Target R-Value &minus; Existing R-Value (e.g., R-49 &minus; R-19 = R-30 needed)</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3: Lookup Bag Yield</strong> (AttiCat R-30 top-off yields 62.5 sq ft / bag)</p>
        <p><strong>Step 4: Calculate Bag Count</strong> = 1,000 sq ft &divide; 62.5 = 16 bags</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">4. Essential Accessories &amp; Preparation</h2>
      <p>Before blowing insulation, you must install key safety accessories to protect your roof and home:</p>

      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Rafter Vent Baffles (Soffit Baffles):</strong> Staple polystyrene rafter vents into every rafter bay along eaves. This prevents blown insulation from clogging soffit intake vents, ensuring continuous airflow.</li>
        <li><strong>Recessed Light Covers / Can Covers:</strong> Non-IC rated recessed lights must be covered with fire-rated covers or kept 3 inches clear of insulation to prevent fire hazard.</li>
        <li><strong>Attic Hatch Insulated Box:</strong> Build a 2" XPS foam box over your attic access stairs or hatch panel so heat does not escape through the door.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Bottom Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="mt-10 p-6 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl text-center">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Ready to Calculate Your Attic Insulation Order?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mb-4">Use our free attic insulation calculator to get instant bag counts, machine rental hours, and soffit baffle requirements.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/attic-insulation" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-8 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Open Attic Insulation Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
